WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.560
- So the question is can
President Trump pardon

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.410
all of the people who
participated in the Capitol Riots

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:07.840
and laid siege to the Capitol?

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.530
Well, unfortunately, the answer to that

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:11.000
is almost certainly yes

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.290
if President Trump remains the President.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.420
This is probably one
of the primary reasons

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.960
why it would be a good idea to impeach

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.360
and remove him before
he finishes out his term

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.840
to prevent this and other

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.170
all sorts of really terrible pardons.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:25.220
While the Constitution
does say the President

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.070
can't pardon people in
terms of impeachment,

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.900
this probably is not what that clause

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.030
of the Constitution means.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.250
So yeah, there's probably
nothing that would prevent

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.990
a sitting President Trump from
pardoning all the rioters.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:37.890
Now, of course, that's for federal crimes,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.740
but unfortunately, the President's
pardon power does extend

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.320
to District of Columbia crimes as well.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.620
So stay tuned for more
legal election coverage,

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.863
and as always, I'll see you in court.

